### What does throw early mean?

Throw early means validating inputs at the service entry point and failing immediately with a clear exception. This produces clean stack traces and meaningful error messages.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Objects;
import java.util.Optional;

public class ThrowEarlyDemo {

    // WRONG: throw late. Null travels deep before failing.
    public static void wrongThrowLate(String tradeId) {
        // tradeId could be null. No check here.
        String normalized = tradeId.trim();  // NPE here if null!
        System.out.println("Normalized: " + normalized);
    }

    // CORRECT: throw early. Validate at entry.
    public static void correctThrowEarly(String tradeId) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(tradeId, "tradeId must not be null");
        if (tradeId.isBlank()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("tradeId must not be blank");
        }
        String normalized = tradeId.trim();
        System.out.println("Normalized: " + normalized);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            correctThrowEarly("  TRD-001  ");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        try {
            correctThrowEarly(null);
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught early: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Normalized: TRD-001
Caught early: tradeId must not be null
```

### What does catch late mean?

Catch late means do not catch in the service layer. Let exceptions propagate to the boundary (controller, listener) that has the context to decide the response.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class CatchLateDemo {

    // WRONG: service catches and returns null
    static String wrongCatchEarly(String tradeId) {
        try {
            if (tradeId.equals("BAD")) throw new RuntimeException("Not found");
            return "SETTLED";
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            return null;  // Caller has NO idea why it failed
        }
    }

    // CORRECT: service throws, boundary catches
    static String correctThrowUp(String tradeId) {
        if (tradeId.equals("BAD")) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Trade not found: " + tradeId);
        }
        return "SETTLED";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Wrong: controller gets null, no context
        String result1 = wrongCatchEarly("BAD");
        System.out.println("Wrong: result is " + result1 + " (why? no idea)\n");

        // Correct: controller catches with full context
        try {
            String result2 = correctThrowUp("BAD");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("Correct: controller catches - " + e.getMessage());
            System.out.println("Can return: 404 NOT_FOUND with meaningful message");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong: result is null (why? no idea)

Correct: controller catches - Trade not found: BAD
Can return: 404 NOT_FOUND with meaningful message
```

### How does this create clean layering?

Each layer has a clear responsibility: validate, throw, or catch.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

public class CleanLayeringDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Clean Exception Layering:");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Repository layer:");
        System.out.println("    - Throws DataAccessException (Spring wraps JDBC)");
        System.out.println("    - Does NOT catch");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Service layer:");
        System.out.println("    - Validates early (throw IllegalArgumentException)");
        System.out.println("    - Wraps in domain exception (SettlementException)");
        System.out.println("    - Does NOT catch and return null");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Controller layer:");
        System.out.println("    - ZERO try-catch");
        System.out.println("    - @ControllerAdvice catches late");
        System.out.println("    - Maps to HTTP: 404, 400, 500");
    }
}
```

```text
Clean Exception Layering:

  Repository layer:
    - Throws DataAccessException (Spring wraps JDBC)
    - Does NOT catch

  Service layer:
    - Validates early (throw IllegalArgumentException)
    - Wraps in domain exception (SettlementException)
    - Does NOT catch and return null

  Controller layer:
    - ZERO try-catch
    - @ControllerAdvice catches late
    - Maps to HTTP: 404, 400, 500
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Kafka/SQS listeners.** These are "catch late" boundaries. Catch Exception, decide: retry, send to DLQ, or skip with logging.
- **Batch processors.** Catch per-record, log, accumulate errors, continue processing. Report all failures at the end.
- **Optional.orElseThrow().** The modern "throw early" pattern: `repo.findById(id).orElseThrow(() -> new NotFoundException(id))`.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the anti-pattern of catching too early? (Service catches and returns null/error code. Controller has no context.)
- What is the anti-pattern of throwing too late? (Null propagates deep, NPE in the wrong place with misleading trace.)
- Where should you catch in a REST API? (In @ControllerAdvice, not in the controller method itself.)
